menu "M5Stack Tab5 Example Configuration"

    config M5STACK_TAB5_EXAMPLE_RENDER_TO_FRAME_BUFFERS
        bool "Render LVGL straight into the panel's frame buffers"
        depends on M5STACK_TAB5_DPI_NUM_FBS > 1
        default n
        help
            Initialize the display with
            DisplayConfig::render_to_frame_buffers_enabled: LVGL renders in DIRECT
            mode straight into the DPI panel's frame buffers and a finished
            frame is shown by switching the panel to it, instead of copying
            LVGL's draw buffers into the one frame buffer the panel scans
            out. Saves the two draw buffers and the rotation scratch buffer
            (PSRAM) and the copy on every flush. Needs
            CONFIG_M5STACK_TAB5_DPI_NUM_FBS >= 2 (M5Stack Tab5 Configuration).
            The rotate button does nothing in this mode: LVGL cannot rotate in
            DIRECT mode.

    config M5STACK_TAB5_EXAMPLE_FLUSH_TO_BACK_BUFFER
        bool "Flush LVGL into the back frame buffer (tear-free, rotation works)"
        depends on M5STACK_TAB5_DPI_NUM_FBS > 1 && !M5STACK_TAB5_EXAMPLE_RENDER_TO_FRAME_BUFFERS
        default n
        help
            Initialize the display with
            DisplayConfig::flush_to_back_buffer_enabled: every LVGL flush is
            copied (and rotated) by the PPA into the frame buffer the panel is
            not scanning out, and the panel switches to it at the frame
            boundary once the frame is complete. Partial frames are never on
            screen, so the draw buffers can be strips (see
            M5STACK_TAB5_EXAMPLE_DRAW_BUFFER_LINES) and a full-screen redraw
            still appears at once. The rotate button works. Needs
            CONFIG_M5STACK_TAB5_DPI_NUM_FBS >= 2 (M5Stack Tab5 Configuration).

    config M5STACK_TAB5_EXAMPLE_DRAW_BUFFER_LINES
        int "Rows per LVGL draw buffer"
        depends on !M5STACK_TAB5_EXAMPLE_RENDER_TO_FRAME_BUFFERS
        range 8 1280
        default 1280
        help
            Height, in panel rows of 720 pixels, of each of LVGL's two draw
            buffers; 1280 is a whole frame (1.8 MB of PSRAM each). With one
            frame buffer a full-screen redraw flushes strip by strip into the
            buffer on screen, so a whole-frame draw buffer is what makes it
            appear at once; with the back-buffer flush the strips are
            assembled off screen and a far smaller draw buffer (e.g. 128 rows,
            184 KB, the BSP's default strip) looks the same and leaves the
            PSRAM to the application.

endmenu
